1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
I'm Alex Villarreal with the VOA Special English Technology Report.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:14,000
Builders in developing countries are often not required to build strong buildings.

3
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:21,000
So, when a disaster strikes, the damage is often widespread.

4
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:28,000
Yet Japan is one of the most developed countries in the world.

5
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:38,000
Still, the March 11 earthquake and tsunami waves destroyed more than 14,000 buildings.

6
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:46,000
Brady Cox is an assistant professor of civil engineering at the University of Arkansas.

7
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:58,000
He is also an earthquake expert with an organization called Geotechnical Extreme Events Reconnaissance or Gear.

8
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:02,000
The group studies major disasters.

9
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:11,000
Professor Cox says Japan has one of the best building code systems in the world.

10
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:21,000
However, he says this earthquake was huge, one of the top five earthquakes in recorded history.

11
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:28,000
So, anytime there is an earthquake that large, there is going to be damage.

12
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:32,000
The Quake measured magnitude 9.

13
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:43,000
He says one thing many people don't understand is that building codes are meant to prevent loss of life in earthquakes.

14
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:49,000
That doesn't mean that the buildings won't have major damage.

15
00:01:49,000 --> 00:02:05,000
Mr. Cox says Japan has invested a lot in seismic research and design since a magnitude 7.5 earthquake in Nigata in 1964.

16
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:13,000
That same year, a 9.2 quake shook the American state of Alaska.

17
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:23,000
He says those two earthquakes opened up a lot of new research on something called soil liquefaction.

18
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:36,000
Soil liquefaction is the process by which the strength or stiffness of soil is weakened by an event like the shaking of an earthquake.

19
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:40,000
The soil begins to move like liquid.

20
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:49,000
Professor Cox says the first step to designing an earthquake-resistant building is to study the soil.

21
00:02:49,000 --> 00:03:02,000
Then, the structural engineers take that information and decide the details of the construction, such as, is this going to be a steel structure?

22
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:06,000
Is it going to be reinforced concrete?

23
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:11,000
How will the framing of the building be designed?

24
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:21,000
A team from Geotechnical Extreme Events Reconnaissance is going to Japan to examine the destruction.

25
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:28,000
Mr. Cox says they will also be working on rebuilding efforts.

26
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:39,000
They want to make sure that schools, hospitals, police and fire stations and government buildings are rebuilt well.

27
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:52,000
Mr. Cox and other members of gear went to Haiti after the powerful earthquake last year and continued to work with Haitian officials.

28
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:58,000
For VOA Special English, I'm Alex Villarreal.

